      "writtenOn": "2017-08-11T19:57:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure if this is right assumption to do.\n\nFirst off this compares between two different cluster.  \nSo we will wake up big cluster idle CPU rather than packing on a little CPU.\n\nAlso, even if best_idle_cpu and target_cpu are in the same cluster, if target_cpu is driving frequency to X index already, placing new task on the the idle CPU won\u0027t drop the frequency.\nInstead, the best_idle CPU will be on X index once the task placed with target_cpu on X.\nIn that case we\u0027re better to pack tasks on target_cpu and keep idle cpu in idle.\n\nIf we want this kind of logic in task placement, algorithm should get with and without cap_idx and go for idle CPU *only* when cap_idx increases when target_cpu is used under *a big assumption* that such decision is always accepted by energy model.\nI\u0027m not sure if such assumption is right thing to do either.\n\nMy patch at [1] is slightly different because of these reasons.  \nSo it compares only between idle cpus in the same cluster.\n\nFYI, all energy_diff() and cap_idx calculation are broken with WALT.  So for any of these with WALT, we should have [2] and [3].\n\n[1] : https://android-review.googlesource.com/#/c/kernel/common/+/454647/\n[2] : https://android-review.googlesource.com/#/c/kernel/common/+/454644/\n[3] : https://android-review.googlesource.com/#/c/kernel/common/+/454645/",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6511,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 6511,
        "endChar": 44
      },
      "revId": "09c339096a04122c752d792a499d907dbd574a29",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5b1491d2_6232f65e",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/fair.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 6511,
      "author": {
        "id": 1124848
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-08-11T20:19:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Also, even if best_idle_cpu and target_cpu are in the same cluster, if target_cpu is driving frequency to X index already, placing new task on the the idle CPU won\u0027t drop the frequency.\nInstead, the best_idle CPU will be on X index once the task placed with target_cpu on X.\nIn that case we\u0027re better to pack tasks on target_cpu and keep idle cpu in idle.\n\nAbout this point, I\u0027m not too sure (or maybe I missed something). Placing task on target_cpu (which already has X) will increase its frequency from X to Y. Instead placing it on an idle CPU in the same cluster will make the overall cluster frequency to be lower, than it would have been if you did task packing onto the active target CPU.",
      "parentUuid": "f6e98a5c_b34c18a0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6511,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 6511,
        "endChar": 44
      },
      "revId": "09c339096a04122c752d792a499d907dbd574a29",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2fcda8a0_bbeef277",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/fair.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 6511,
      "author": {
        "id": 1209348
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-08-11T21:07:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e About this point, I\u0027m not too sure (or maybe I missed something).\n \u003e Placing task on target_cpu (which already has X) will increase its\n \u003e frequency from X to Y.\n\nThis isn\u0027t always true.  The target cpu\u0027s load could be already high enough to drive Y without new task\u0027s load.\nFor example, we have idle CPU 0 and loaded CPU 1.  Say CPU 1 is at idx 2.  If the new task is small enough, placing the new task on CPU 1 won\u0027t change idx.\nThis change doesn\u0027t consider such index difference caused by new task.  \nRather, in above case it will wake up CPU 0 which will be running at idx 2 actually because load at CPU 1 even though calculated cap_idx of best_idle_cpu (CPU 0) was 0.\nConsequently we will be running 2 CPUs at idx 2.",
      "parentUuid": "5b1491d2_6232f65e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6511,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 6511,
        "endChar": 44
      },
      "revId": "09c339096a04122c752d792a499d907dbd574a29",
